    go test -run TestHealthEndpointCommit ./cmd/server/
      -> FAIL main_test.go:80: Expected body to contain "\"commit\":\"sentinel-health-endpoint-commit\"" ...
         Body: {"status":"ok"}   (EXIT=1)
M2  delete the X-Budget2-Build r.Use middleware block from cmd/server/main.go:
    go test -run TestXBudget2BuildHeader ./cmd/server/
      -> FAIL main_test.go:96: X-Budget2-Build = "", want "sentinel-build-header-commit"   (EXIT=1)
M3  delete `Commit: Commit,` from version.Get():
    go test -run 'TestGet$|TestGet_CommitJSONMarshaling' ./internal/version/
      -> FAIL version_test.go:34 (expected Info.Commit ... got "") AND
         FAIL version_test.go:53 (expected "sentinel-commit-xyz", got "")   (EXIT=1)
M4b replace version.Commit with a hard-coded literal in get_status:
    go test -run TestGetStatusReportsVersionAndCommit ./internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/
      -> FAIL status_test.go:36: commit = "hardcoded-literal", want "sentinel-status-commit"   (EXIT=1)
M4c' perturb the health value (version.Commit -> version.Commit + "X"):
    -> FAIL handlers_test.go:114: expected "sentinel-health-commit", got "sentinel-health-commitX" (EXIT=1)
    -> FAIL TestHealthEndpointCommit in cmd/server (EXIT=1)
M5  remove the Commit ldflag from the Makefile:
    make -n build -> ldflags no longer contain any `version.Commit=` term.
The brief's "track the variable, not a literal" requirement is therefore mechanically enforced:
M4b/M4c' show a hard-coded or perturbed value is caught by the sentinel-injection assertions.

