== FINDINGS (non-blocking; for the lead's backlog, not FAILs) ==
F1  No committed test pins the two "no fallback" contracts: (a) the default `Commit == "unknown"`
    in a bare `go test`/`go build`, and (b) `Get()` must not fall back to vcs.revision.
    TestGet_WithVCSSettings injects vcs.revision but asserts nothing about info.Commit, so a
    future fallback would be added silently. The brief's Tests section did not require such a
    test, so this is not a criterion miss. I proved the behavior with throwaway probes
    (TestProbe_BareBuildCommitUnknown / TestProbe_NoFallbackToVCSRevision, reproduced verbatim in
    this verdict). Candidate for a V3-pattern test-only follow-up so the evidence outlives this file.
F2  Three docs still assert the old health body and were out of the brief's named scope
    (the brief named only handlers_live.go):
      docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-09-whatif-mcp-live-page.md:723, :806, :872
      docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-09-whatif-mcp-live-page-design.md:216
    These are historical plan/spec records, so leaving them is defensible; flagging for the backlog.
F3  Observation only: /api/health's key order is now {"commit":...,"status":"ok"} (Go sorts
    map[string]string keys). Any consumer doing exact-string equality on the body rather than a
    substring/JSON-decode would break. I enumerated the consumers (above) and found none;
    cmd/validate uses `contains`, and its test passes.
